# journalctl — real-world examples

## Actively follow the log (like tail -f)

```sh
journalctl -f
```

## Display all errors since last boot

```sh
journalctl -b -p err
```

## Filter by time period

```sh
journalctl --since=2026-07-01 --until="2026-07-02 23:59:59"
journalctl --since '10:00' --until '11:00'
```

## Show list of systemd units logged in the journal

```sh
journalctl -F _SYSTEMD_UNIT
```

## Filter by unit, executable, PID, command, or user

```sh
journalctl -u dbus                 # by unit
journalctl /usr/bin/dbus-daemon    # by executable path
journalctl _PID=123                # by PID
journalctl _COMM=sshd              # by command name
journalctl _UID=1000               # by user ID
journalctl _COMM=crond --since '10:00' --until '11:00'
```

## List all available boots

```sh
journalctl --list-boots
journalctl -b -1        # the previous boot
```

## Homelab: follow Plex logs on mediaserver

```sh
ssh david@mediaserver 'journalctl -u plexmediaserver -f'
```

## Homelab: why did homelab reboot last night?

```sh
journalctl -b -1 -p err --no-pager | tail -50
```

## Homelab: keep the journal from eating the disk

```sh
journalctl --disk-usage
sudo journalctl --vacuum-size=500M    # trim archived logs to 500MB
```

## Homelab: kernel messages only (like dmesg with timestamps)

```sh
journalctl -k -b
```

| Flag          | What it does                        |
| ------------- | ----------------------------------- |
| -f            | follow new entries                  |
| -u <unit>     | filter by systemd unit              |
| -b            | current boot (-b -1 = previous)     |
| -p err        | priority filter (err, warning, ...) |
| -k            | kernel messages only                |
| --since/--until | time window                       |
| --no-pager    | plain output, no less               |
| -r            | newest entries first                |
